ndary of the input point set.", "delaunay.coords": "This subsection documents the stored input coordinates in flat x y order. It notes the array keeps the constructor type or uses Float64Array when built with the from helper.", "delaunay.update()": "This subsection describes updating the triangulation in place after modifying coordinate values to avoid extra allocations. It highlights the benefit for iterative relaxation methods such as Lloyds algorithm.", "Performance": "This section presents benchmark timings against seven other JavaScript Delaunay libraries on several point distributions and sizes. It shows Delaunator is substantially faster across uniform, gauss, grid and degenerate cases.", "Papers": "This section lists the three publications that inspired the algorithm. It cites sweep-line and radial sweep-hull triangulation methods published between 2010 and 2013.", "Robustness": "This section states that Delaunator produces valid output even for highly degenerate input. It credits the robust-predicates dependency as a modern port of Shewchuks standard geometric predicates.", "Ports to other languages": "This section lists community ports of Delaunator to many other languages and platforms. It includes implementations for Rust, Go, C++, C#, Ruby, Python, Torch, Kotlin, Java, Stata Mata and Julia."}, "concerns": [], "rationale": "Read README.md with read_file and summarized all headings except Example with two sentences each"}

